{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Convert staged Latin-1 filenames into UTF-8 paths under converted/, retaining directory structure and file contents.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The two non-colliding Latin-1 names were created with correct UTF-8 byte sequences. Direct comparisons and matching SHA-256 hashes show that both files retained their source contents; the captured staged tree was flat, so its directory structure was retained.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","cmd-dfe6023aa57a4f75","cmd-81a42c88d6d64d07","cmd-314c243c94164179","cmd-4cf8043560c54e8c"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Preserve the imported tree.","status":"satisfied","summary":"All three original imported files remained present after conversion and restart, with hashes matching the preparation baseline.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","cmd-dfe6023aa57a4f75","cmd-314c243c94164179","cmd-4cf8043560c54e8c"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Report destination collisions without overwriting the existing destination or silently dropping the source.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The plain.txt collision was recorded in conversion-collisions.txt. The source remained present, and the existing destination retained its baseline SHA-256 hash through the restart.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","cmd-81a42c88d6d64d07","cmd-314c243c94164179","cmd-c120ec34948c474f","cmd-4cf8043560c54e8c"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"CentOS 5.11 and kernel 2.6.18-398.el5 were unchanged after execution and restart. The successful conversion used the platform's installed Python 2.4 runtime.","evidence_ids":["cmd-eb33e3a3b5fb4434","cmd-81a42c88d6d64d07","cmd-4cf8043560c54e8c","global:after_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform"]}],"confidence":0.99,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.94,"summary":"No unnecessary managed-system mutation was demonstrated: the created converted files and collision report served the task, and the reboot served the mandatory evidence protocol. Failed Python attempts either stopped at parsing/import or encountered permission denial, with no evidence of state change. One minor executor-caused residue remained: cmd-3e3f02234b03432f redirected reboot output to /tmp/antrieb-reboot.log, and the after-executor snapshot shows that temporary file was not cleaned up.","evidence_ids":["cmd-6b7f00c26f274e3a","cmd-bbdbf0f3156345b0","cmd-18dbc30ff00e4700","cmd-b8362e9924354d52","cmd-3e3f02234b03432f","global:after_prepare:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct final-state evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol is also complete: a healthy pre-restart state and boot identity were captured, the restart was acknowledged and downtime observed, a different boot identity appeared afterward, and SSH, platform identity, converted data, original sources, and collision handling all recovered successfully. Fixed lifecycle boot observations independently corroborate the transition.","limitations":["The legacy node does not support the lifecycle collector's systemd failed-unit check, and its package-health probe invoked an unsupported yum command; neither creates contradictory evidence for the task outcomes.","Operational hygiene is slightly reduced by the retained /tmp/antrieb-reboot.log file."]}
